WHY THE COPYRIGHT LINE IS HERE AND NOT IN PER-FILE HEADERS

Apache-2.0 is normally applied by adding a short copyright header to each source
file. That is deliberately NOT done here.

The source files in code/ are the object of study of the accompanying paper. The
paper's claims are tied to specific file and line ranges through
VERIFICATION_REPORT.md, and the paper states that the artifact is presented as
the state that was analysed rather than as a deliverable to be improved. Inserting
headers would shift every line number, break that correspondence, and edit the
very thing the paper asks a reader to inspect unchanged.

The NOTICE file is Apache-2.0's own mechanism for exactly this situation: a
project-level attribution that applies to the distribution without modifying its
contents. LICENSE is a pristine, unmodified copy of the canonical Apache-2.0 text
(verified byte-equivalent against https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt),
including its instructional appendix. The bracketed placeholder inside that
appendix is part of the standard licence text and is intentionally left as-is;
editing it would make the file diverge from canonical Apache-2.0 and stop
automated licence scanners from recognising it.

